\title{Status of the proof that \code{\verb~|expf~} is correctly rounded}
\taxon{Note}
\date{2026-11-23}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{schrodingerzy}
\tag{exp}
\tag{status}
\meta{updated}{2026-11-24}
\meta{pr}{17}
\import{base-macros}

\p{Updated on 2026-11-24 in session [ses-ZHIG](ses-ZHIG) (\pr{14}–\pr{17}, implementing [dec-03IY](dec-03IY)). Replaces the legacy LLVM-IR interpreter and structured-form bridge description from \pr{13} with the direct Clang-AST extraction and multi-plan proof state.}

\subtree[note-expf-status-the-statement]{
\title{The statements}

\p{\code{\verb~|LibcMath/Extracted/Statements.lean~} states \code{\verb~|CorrectlyRounded (Spec.exp .binary32)~} directly on total AST-extracted functional Lean kernels (\code{\verb~|LibcMath/Extracted/Expf/*.lean~}, produced by \code{\verb~|ast2lean~} from upstream LLVM libc commit \code{\verb~|e1a5f92db023~} with Clang 23.1.5):}

\ul{
\li{\code{\verb~|ExpfCorrectlyRounded~} (= \code{\verb~|ExpfDoubleEvalCorrectlyRounded~}): \code{\verb~|math::double_eval::expf~} (default x86-64, \code{\verb~|LIBC_MATH=0~}, no hardware FMA) across all four C rounding directions.}
\li{\code{\verb~|ExpfDoubleEvalFmaCorrectlyRounded~}: \code{\verb~|math::double_eval::expf~} compiled with \code{\verb~|-mavx2 -mfma~} across all four C rounding directions.}
\li{\code{\verb~|ExpfStaticRoundingCorrectlyRounded~}: \code{\verb~|math::static_rounding::expf~} (pure integer \code{\verb~|Frac64~}, \code{\verb~|LIBC_MATH=0x0C~}) across all four C rounding directions.}
\li{\code{\verb~|ExpfIntegerEvalCorrectlyRounded~}: \code{\verb~|math::integer_eval::expf~} (pure integer \code{\verb~|Frac64~}, \code{\verb~|LIBC_MATH=0x2C~}) in \code{\verb~|.nearest~}.}
\li{\code{\verb~|ExpfFloatEvalCrRnCorrectlyRounded~}: \code{\verb~|math::float_eval_cr_rn::expf~} (GPU \code{\verb~|FloatFloat~} FMA, \code{\verb~|LIBC_MATH=0x3E~}) in \code{\verb~|.nearest~}.}
}

\p{Additionally, \code{\verb~|Expf.FloatEval~} (\code{\verb~|LIBC_MATH=0x3F~}, fast ~1 ULP single-precision Estrin+Horner) is extracted for cross-plan diagnostics and differential testing.}
}

\subtree[note-expf-status-what-is-proved]{
\title{What is proved}

\p{\code{\verb~|LibcMath/Extracted/ExpfMain.lean~} reduces correct rounding directly to the two finite polynomial-evaluation ranges with \strong{zero} \code{\verb~|sorry~}, \strong{zero} interpreter indirection, and \strong{zero} open bridge hypotheses (\code{\verb~|Block15ComputesExpfMain~} in [prob-W1QC](prob-W1QC) is superseded and removed). Every top-level theorem carries a \code{\verb~|#guard_msgs in #print axioms~} check confirming dependence only on \code{\verb~|[propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]~}:}

\table{
\tr{\th{plan}\th{positive main range}\th{negative main range}\th{remaining / mode}\th{discharged analytically}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|DoubleEval~} / \code{\verb~|DoubleEvalFma~}}\td{\code{\verb~|0x32800001~}–\code{\verb~|0x42B17217~}}\td{\code{\verb~|0xB2800001~}–\code{\verb~|0xC2CFF1B4~}}\td{545 350 603}\td{87.30\%}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|StaticRounding~} / \code{\verb~|IntegerEval~}}\td{\code{\verb~|0x33000000~}–\code{\verb~|0x42B17217~}}\td{\code{\verb~|0xB3000000~}–\code{\verb~|0xC2CFF1B4~}}\td{528 573 389}\td{87.69\%}}
}

\p{In addition, \code{\verb~|ExpfPaths.integerEval_eq_staticRounding_nearest~} proves pointwise equality of \code{\verb~|IntegerEval.kernel .nearest~} and \code{\verb~|StaticRounding.kernel .nearest~} across all \code{\verb~|2^32~} inputs, so \code{\verb~|expfIntegerEvalCorrectlyRounded_of_staticRounding~} derives RN correctness of \code{\verb~|IntegerEval~} directly from \code{\verb~|StaticRounding~}.}

\p{The supporting library consists of:}
\ul{
\li{\code{\verb~|LibcMath/Core/~}: FloatLib-backed LLVM libc core operations, \code{\verb~|MathConfig~}, \code{\verb~|KernelMeta~}, \code{\verb~|UnaryKernel~}, polynomial DSL, and \code{\verb~|Core.Diag~}.}
\li{\code{\verb~|LibcMath/Functions/ExpRanges.lean~}, \code{\verb~|AddRounding.lean~}, \code{\verb~|AddSpecial.lean~}: closed forms of \code{\verb~|Spec.exp~} and IEEE addition.}
\li{\code{\verb~|LibcMath/Extracted/ExpfPaths/{Exec,Ranges}.lean~}: direct symbolic simplification of the extracted functions on their short paths.}
\li{\code{\verb~|LibcMath/Exhaustive/~} and \code{\verb~|LibcMath/Oracle/Exp.lean~}: total-function range reduction and sound \code{\verb~|expOracle~}.}
}
}

\subtree[note-expf-status-what-is-left]{
\title{What is left}

\p{Discharging the remaining polynomial ranges requires checking each main-range block against \code{\verb~|expOracle~} (and generating Lean certificates via \code{\verb~|scripts/exhaustive.py~}), or proving an analytical error bound directly on the extracted functional expressions. Measured costs with the direct AST-extracted kernels ([ins-FH1S](ins-FH1S)):}

\table{
\tr{\th{method}\th{trust base}\th{measured speed}}
\tr{\td{compiled \code{\verb~|checkrange~}}\td{report only, not a proof}\td{4–8 ms per 512 inputs (8–16 µs / input)}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|decide +kernel~} (\code{\verb~|Kernel/Demo~})}\td{Lean kernel only}\td{4.56 s wall / 3.60 s user for 32 inputs incl. startup}}
}
}

\subtree[note-expf-status-what-the-theorem-rests-on]{
\title{What the theorem rests on and empirical validation}

\p{Beyond Lean's kernel and standard Mathlib axioms, the claim that LLVM libc's machine code matches the theorem rests on: (1) \code{\verb~|Spec.exp~} and FloatLib's IEEE 754 semantics; (2) faithful AST lifting of C++ expressions and preprocessor \code{\verb~|LIBC_MATH_*~}/\code{\verb~|LIBC_TARGET_CPU_HAS_*~} macros by \code{\verb~|ast2lean~}; and (3) C++ compiler codegen and hardware IEEE 754 execution.}

\p{\code{\verb~|scripts/difftest-expf.sh~} empirically tests (2) and (3) against compiled Clang x86-64 machine code across all five C++ entrypoint plans. Dense differential runs (\code{\verb~|step=2003~}, 259 704 main-range inputs per mode; plus \code{\verb~|quick~} boundary sweeps) reported \strong{0 mismatches} across 2.85M total evaluations: \code{\verb~|double_eval~} (1 038 816 across 4 modes), \code{\verb~|double_eval_fma~} (1 038 816 across 4 modes), \code{\verb~|integer_eval~} (259 704 in RN), \code{\verb~|float_eval~} (259 704 in RN), and \code{\verb~|float_eval_cr_rn~} (259 704 in RN).}
}
